WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:11.500
Day in, day out you are using one of the biggest
machines in the world and may not even know

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:12.500
it.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:16.230
It’s in your home, it lines the streets
and it’s powered by gigantic engines across

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.230
the country.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:20.880
The biggest machine in the world isn’t this,
or this, or even this.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.460
It’s the electrical power-grid or more specifically
the US power grid.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:29.769
In the United States alone the grid contains
enough high voltage wiring to wrap around

00:00:29.769 --> 00:00:34.370
the earth 6 times over and that’s not including
all the low voltage wiring running through

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:36.480
our streets and into our homes.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.410
This entire system was built in a little over
100 years and since then we have become very

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:45.690
accustomed to having electricity available
to us at the flip of a switch, but before

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:50.430
this machine existed our only sources of light
by night were lanterns and candles and nearly

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:53.190
all our daily chores needed to be done by
hand.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:57.220
This giant machine started pretty small with
the world’s first central power station

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:00.180
built on Pearl Street, Manhattan in 1882.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:05.269
Thomas Edison, the ever present American businessman,
saw an opportunity to not only sell more of

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:08.790
his lightbulbs, but to sell the electricity
to power to them.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:13.320
So he built an electric generator driven by
a coal powered steam engine to create electricity

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.490
at a cost that would allow his light bulbs
to compete with lanterns.

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:21.580
The station contained 6 of these generators
each produced 100 kilowatts, enough to power

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:27.820
1200 of edison's lightbulbs, allowing edison
to sell 7200 lightbulbs, which needed to be

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:32.560
replaced regularly, as these early bulbs lasted
about 1200 hours.

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:36.590
Even then the project ran an operating loss
for it’s first 2 years.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:39.299
Setting up this system was a bold business
move.

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:44.170
The machines, the conduits and the 30 kilometres
of wiring needed for it were expensive at

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:48.780
a cost of 300,000 dollars or about 20 million
dollars in today’s money.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:52.640
The expensive Lower Manhattan real estate
didn’t help either, but Edison’s placement

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:57.210
of the Pearl Street Station was vital to the
success of his idea, as it had the population

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:01.700
density to make the project financially viable,
but he also made one of his first customers

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.380
The New York Times.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:07.619
Who published a story about the historic event,
reporting that the light was “soft, mellow

00:02:07.619 --> 00:02:11.019
and graceful to the eye…without a flicker
to make the headache.”

00:02:11.019 --> 00:02:15.420
This was all the marketing Edison needed and
demand for the system grew, but this pointed

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:17.520
out one of the systems major flaws.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:22.220
It couldn’t scale economically without installing
more power stations nearby, because it generated

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:27.000
a low voltage direct current and to efficiently
transfer power over long distances we need

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.020
to transform the low voltage to a higher voltage.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:33.709
The power loss equation states the power lost
in transmission equals the current squared

00:02:33.709 --> 00:02:37.670
multiplied by the resistance of the wire and
so to reduce power loss we really need to

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:39.410
minimise the current.

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:44.209
This equation describes the relationship between
power transmitted and the voltage and current.

00:02:44.209 --> 00:02:48.340
The community demand fixes the amount of power
required and so to decrease the current we

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:50.740
need increase the transmission voltage.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:54.209
Alternating current’s main advantage over
direct current isn’t that it loses less

00:02:54.209 --> 00:02:58.910
power over long distance, but that it can
be transformed from low voltage to high voltage

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:01.450
much more efficiently than direct current.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:05.760
Thus as demand for power grew across the nation
the famed argument between Edison’s idea

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:10.900
to have many smaller generators located near
population centres and Nicolas Tesla’s idea

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:15.390
of having one large efficient engine located
far from population centres grew.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:19.710
Of-course we know who won and today we have
hundreds of gigantic power stations located

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:23.630
far from population centres generating gigawatts
of power.

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:27.130
So what would a map of the current US electrical
grid look like?

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:30.980
Let’s create a map showing the largest power
plants across the US.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:35.280
In areas where rainfall is high like Washington
state on the west coast and Alabama in the

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.520
south there is a high volume of electricity
generated with hydropower.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.450
Where wind speeds are highest in central America
there is a growing number of wind turbines

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:48.290
and states that get the most sun like california
and Arizona have the highest percentage of

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:52.700
solar power, there are several high power
nuclear power plants located in areas where

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:57.470
water is plentiful, which is needed for the
steam turbines and to cool their cores.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:02.140
Coal power plants tend to be close to coal
mines as its a very heavy fuel, but The largest

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:06.830
percentage of energy in the US is generated
from natural gas, as it is the cleanest of

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:08.020
the fossil fuels.

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:12.070
Let’s zoom in on one of these power plants
to get an idea of how this energy is delivered

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:13.930
to population centres.

00:04:13.930 --> 00:04:18.739
As I mentioned earlier it is vital that electricity
is transmitted at high voltage, so the first

00:04:18.739 --> 00:04:23.490
step as the electricity exits the power plant
is to pass it through a step-up transformer,

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:27.560
the electricity is then delivered across these
high voltage power lines that you see stretching

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:28.930
across the country.

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:33.380
These power lines feed into local step-down
transformers for local distribution in neighbourhoods

00:04:33.380 --> 00:04:37.880
and gets stepped down again by these transformer
drums on poles outside your house.

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:41.710
The electricity you consume in your home is
measured on a meter like this that reads the

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:44.250
power running through the wire that enters
your house.

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:48.220
So let’s say after a long day’s work you
come home and decide you want to watch the

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:49.949
premiere episode of Game of Thrones.

00:04:49.949 --> 00:04:54.639
You plug in your viewing device of choice
and that meter starts to tick a little faster,

00:04:54.639 --> 00:04:57.310
along with millions of other meters across
the nation.

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:01.730
Suddenly the biggest machine in the world
just got a little bigger and the engines powering

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:04.710
it have to work harder to keep up with that
demand.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:08.100
This is one of the huge disadvantages of the
current power-grid.

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.440
During low demand hours we have a number of
always on power plants called baseload plants,

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:16.419
that are powered by reliable energy sources
like coal and nuclear.

00:05:16.419 --> 00:05:19.780
These maintain the baseload requirements of
the country, but what happens if one of these

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:21.280
power stations fail?

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.441
Suddenly the system doesn’t have enough
energy to meet demand and if we don’t have

00:05:24.441 --> 00:05:28.710
the ability to meet it power stations will
begin rolling blackouts where they cut the

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:31.810
electricity to certain areas to prevent a
larger blackout.

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:36.870
One of the largest of these rolling blackouts
to ever occur was the 2003 north-eastern blackout

00:05:36.870 --> 00:05:39.120
that left 50 million people without electricity.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:40.120
Rolling blackouts like this should usually
never occur in a country as advanced as the

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:41.120
US.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.620
Modern countries will have many redundant
power sources that can be brought online when

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:48.500
power demand surges or power stations go down
and excellent communication between all parts

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:51.790
of the grid to ensure problems can be fixed
immediately.

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:56.040
But as renewable sources like wind and solar
become more popular this micro-management

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.800
of the power-grid is becoming even more difficult.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.639
We cannot command the weather to make these
power sources work harder when needed and

00:06:02.639 --> 00:06:06.380
the more likely scenario is that we have to
shut down these power sources while they could

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:11.000
be generating energy, because they are generating
energy that is not needed, which can overload

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:12.000
the system.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.320
It’s unlikely that we will be able to rely
on renewable energy without still having many

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:21.140
baseload power plants running on natural gas,
coal or nuclear, unless we create an efficient

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:26.110
way of saving this energy during peak performance
for later use when the demand is there.

00:06:26.110 --> 00:06:31.509
Currently the largest energy storage method
worldwide is hydro-electricity at about 80%.

00:06:31.509 --> 00:06:35.300
On some days of the year Denmark generates
so much energy from Wind that they have enough

00:06:35.300 --> 00:06:39.550
to export to countries like Norway, who often
use the advantage of their mountainous terrain

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:43.320
to buy this cheap excess power to pump water
to the top of mountains.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:47.250
This water can then be released to power hydroelectric
turbines when it’s needed.

00:06:47.250 --> 00:06:51.289
Of-course this leads to some power loss, but
it is better than shutting down the wind turbines

00:06:51.289 --> 00:06:53.040
while they are at peak performance.

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:56.600
This issue is only going to grow as solar
energy becomes more and more popular, which

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:00.890
is why Tesla’s Powerwall is such an integral
part of making widespread home installed solar

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:01.900
power a reality.

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:06.410
If we cannot store the excess energy generated
by these panels during the day, for use when

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:10.610
there isn’t sunlight, then we are still
going to depend highly on those baseload power

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:13.100
plants using fossil fuels or nuclear energy.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:17.110
As the price of manufacturing solar panels
decreases it is inevitable that all countries

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:20.300
will begin to move away from the current model
of the powergrid.

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:24.130
We are at the brink of world changing moment
and the largest machines in the world are

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:26.660
going to drastically change for the better.

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:31.319
Large fossil fuel power-plants generating
AC electricity are quickly becoming out-dated

00:07:31.319 --> 00:07:35.009
and will be gradually decommissioned over
the next couple of decades and a new model

00:07:35.009 --> 00:07:37.449
for the power-grid will rise in it’s place.

00:07:37.449 --> 00:07:41.320
We will be gradually moving towards Thomas
Edison’s early vision of having many local

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:45.210
low voltage power generators creating DC power,
but with a modern twist.

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:49.500
They will all be powered by nothing more than
the energy we can draw from the ever replenishing

00:07:49.500 --> 00:10:05.000
pool that nature has provided us.

